(387101, 32144, 1031, '<td valign="top">Thanks for all the comments -- their consistency is quite gratifying.<br/> <br/>I was riding in the back seat as we were driving at about a 45 degree angle towards the left. I took the shot out the front passenger-side window over my dad''s shoulder. The motion blur combined with a bit of haze, road vibration, and the backlit conditions did in any chance for a sharp photo. But it was the "best" right-angle shot in terms of composition I had in the one day I had between returning from my trip and the entry deadline.<br/><a href="//www.pbase.com/image/20183017" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">One of my outtakes</a> is perhaps a better photo, but I think the "strength" comes from other elements than the right-angles.</td>', 'Thanks for all the comments -- their consistency is quite gratifying. I was riding in the back seat as we were driving at about a 45 degree angle towards the left. I took the shot out the front passenger-side window over my dad''s shoulder. The motion blur combined with a bit of haze, road vibration, and the backlit conditions did in any chance for a sharp photo. But it was the "best" right-angle shot in terms of composition I had in the one day I had between returning from my trip and the entry deadline.One of my outtakes is perhaps a better photo, but I think the "strength" comes from other elements than the right-angles.', '2003-08-13 17:43:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(387137, 31405, 4411, '<td valign="top">Do I like this shot or what?!<br/>Great colours &amp; textures on the metal. The rust really adds to the shot. As David said, the closest bolt could have done to have been in focus, otherwise this is superb.<br/> <br/>Ignore the narrow minded voters - it''s their loss ; )</td>', 'Do I like this shot or what?!Great colours & textures on the metal. The rust really adds to the shot. As David said, the closest bolt could have done to have been in focus, otherwise this is superb. Ignore the narrow minded voters - it''s their loss ; )', '2003-08-13 18:29:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(387145, 31567, 4411, '<td valign="top">If I had to find a fault in this it would be that the face of the cross should be darker! I''m a big fan of silhouttes and this is a good one - the harsh lines of the cross contrast very well against the random chaos of the shrubs. Great shot.</td>', 'If I had to find a fault in this it would be that the face of the cross should be darker! I''m a big fan of silhouttes and this is a good one - the harsh lines of the cross contrast very well against the random chaos of the shrubs. Great shot.', '2003-08-13 18:32:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(387150, 31810, 4411, '<td valign="top">Goddam 10D owners, just look at the state of that background will ya ... absolutely beautiful!<br/>Shocked that this isn''t up in the 6''s where it belongs. Love the composition, love the silhouette, and I can''t bear to look at that b/g any more : )</td>', 'Goddam 10D owners, just look at the state of that background will ya ... absolutely beautiful!Shocked that this isn''t up in the 6''s where it belongs. Love the composition, love the silhouette, and I can''t bear to look at that b/g any more : )', '2003-08-13 18:39:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(387161, 32240, 8865, '<td valign="top">128th? Wow. That''s pretty low. I thought this picture would be up there. I really liked this shot &amp; the humour ;) I gave it a 9. I see right angles everywhere. Anyways, I thought it was an excellent picture and deserves to be higher up. :)</td>', '128th? Wow. That''s pretty low. I thought this picture would be up there. I really liked this shot & the humour ;) I gave it a 9. I see right angles everywhere. Anyways, I thought it was an excellent picture and deserves to be higher up. :)', '2003-08-13 18:45:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(387166, 31669, 4411, '<td valign="top">Not sure exactly why I like this. Must be something to do with the contrasts - the greyness of the landscape and the building surrounded by green, the angular building against the curves of the landscape. Whatever it is it works for me.</td>', 'Not sure exactly why I like this. Must be something to do with the contrasts - the greyness of the landscape and the building surrounded by green, the angular building against the curves of the landscape. Whatever it is it works for me.', '2003-08-13 18:46:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(387180, 31628, 10802, '<td valign="top">thanks for all the comments</td>', 'thanks for all the comments', '2003-08-13 18:56:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(387188, 31402, 1368, '<td valign="top">Cant belive how low this got!! Its beautiful</td>', 'Cant belive how low this got!! Its beautiful', '2003-08-13 19:01:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(387218, 31402, 6405, '<td valign="top">The main point is ,your photo rocks,I like it very much! <br/> <br/>With 1/8 shutter,probably handheld you got perfect focus on cat''s face!</td>', 'The main point is ,your photo rocks,I like it very much!  With 1/8 shutter,probably handheld you got perfect focus on cat''s face!', '2003-08-13 19:19:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(387241, 31338, 6999, '<td valign="top">Damn, Chris I meant to leave a comment on this shot and didn''t - just spottd it whilst browsing through the full results. I was one of your 8''s - and I only rated three photos higher. <br/> <br/>I love the colour and depth here - those repeating layers fading a little as they recede, excellently done. I also think that perhaps more than any other shot this REALLY met the challenge - as in ''the strength of the composition'' etc. : both the odd foreground blur, and the echo of it in the window-frame really pull they eye in. A difficult trick to pull off, and it''s no surprise that most of us went for depictions of right angles rather than employing them as a compositional device.<br/> <br/>There''s a powerful feeling of mystery in this shot too, of a kind of yearning - it reminds me of books like Le Grande Meaulnes, or HG Wells''s green door ... something glimpsed fleetingly and perhaps never to be found again: so a sense of loss also.<br/> <br/>Most good work. Overlooked by the audience here, evidently, but yours is not the only lost photo :-)<br/> <br/>Ed<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-08-13 18:32:17.</i></td>', 'Damn, Chris I meant to leave a comment on this shot and didn''t - just spottd it whilst browsing through the full results. I was one of your 8''s - and I only rated three photos higher.  I love the colour and depth here - those repeating layers fading a little as they recede, excellently done. I also think that perhaps more than any other shot this REALLY met the challenge - as in ''the strength of the composition'' etc. : both the odd foreground blur, and the echo of it in the window-frame really pull they eye in. A difficult trick to pull off, and it''s no surprise that most of us went for depictions of right angles rather than employing them as a compositional device. There''s a powerful feeling of mystery in this shot too, of a kind of yearning - it reminds me of books like Le Grande Meaulnes, or HG Wells''s green door ... something glimpsed fleetingly and perhaps never to be found again: so a sense of loss also. Most good work. Overlooked by the audience here, evidently, but yours is not the only lost photo :-) Ed', '2003-08-13 19:31:06.000000', '2003-08-13 19:32:17.000000', 0),
